6627f4b91c977670cf9f919aff8b659cb5b4776cf3d04bb561b50b79fb.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{19}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{7}{2},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{19}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139535929 17592186045443 true] [17592186543825 1072 #uuid "9be43ff2-1cbd-5518-ad47-108fe13ce12b" 13194139535929 17592186045443 true] [17592186543825 1074 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/fa/fa1022d2a1718627898289abdd4d3035a6e06230eea16d0e46832675b2e37def.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{1}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{3}+(- \\frac{7}{2})+\\frac{19}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-38+(-21)+57}{6} \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535929 17592186045443 true] [17592186543826 1080 "\\frac{11}{2}" 13194139535929 17592186045443 true] [17592186543826 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535929 17592186045443 true] [17592186543826 1083 #uuid "a0015fc8-88b5-5637-806e-0694134e